prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 5–8 sentences: my Quality Gate (fmt + lint + typecheck) and Test Gate are green. The agent-dev loop's Code Review Gate is next — describe exactly how you dispatch it, what context the reviewer sees, what it returns, what happens when the score is below threshold, and what changes when `project.md` declares a project-specific reviewer.
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers six properties of the §7 Code Review Gate. (1) **Position.** Sits between the Test Gate and the Push Gate — not inside Quality, not after Push. Tests must already be green before the reviewer sees the diff. (2) **Clean-context dispatch.** The reviewer runs in a *fresh* agent call (Claude Code's Agent tool, Kiro's equivalent, or Codex's CLI — the contract is portable, the host translates). The reviewer is not the same agent doing the implementation. (3) **What's passed in.** `project.md` (project invariants — architecture, hard constraints, public-repo signal), the diff vs. base, and the reviewer reference + threshold + authority + retry_budget. **What's deliberately excluded.** The feature spec at `feature/<feature>.md`, the Session/Decision Log, the in-progress conversation history. Excluding feature context is the whole point: the reviewer evaluates the diff against the *project*, not against the agent's own plan. (4) **Default reviewer = host-native.** When `project.md` doesn't declare a `code_review:` block (or declares `reviewer: host-native`), the gate dispatches to whatever code review the host coding agent ships built-in. No mAId-shipped generic reviewer. (5) **Configured reviewer is preferred.** When `project.md`'s `## Agent Development > kdevkit > code_review:` block names a reviewer (`skill:<name>` / `mcp:<server>.<tool>` / `agent:<name>`), the gate dispatches to that instead of host-native. The `<ref>` syntax is prefix-tagged; bare strings default to `skill:`. (6) **Sub-threshold loop-back is full-loop.** Reviewer returns findings + 0–100 score. Score ≥ threshold (default 70) → Push Gate. Score < threshold → append findings to the feature spec's Session Log, fix the highest-severity findings as the next slice, and **re-run Quality → Test → Code Review** from the top. Up to `retry_budget` cycles (default 2 — total cycles, not retries on top of an initial pass). After exhausting retry_budget: `authority: hard-stop` (default) refuses Push; `authority: soft` allows Push with residuals appended to Session Log. The §9 internal-marker grep fires against the diff *before* dispatch — the diff leaves the agent's controlled boundary on dispatch, so a leak must be caught before, not after. Wrong answers: leaking the feature spec / session log / decision log / conversation history into the reviewer's context (it must be fresh-context); treating the gate as self-introspection or as a "self-score" instead of a separate-agent call; skipping the gate when tests pass; running the gate inside Quality; looping back only to Quality without re-running Test and Code Review (it's a full-loop); placing the gate after Push instead of before; ignoring `project.md`'s `code_review:` block and defaulting to host-native when a reviewer is declared; mistaking `mcp:<server>.<tool>` for `mcp:<server.tool>`; treating `authority: soft` as equivalent to "no budget" (it still uses retry_budget).
tools: claude,kiro
